<!--
  navigate_to_pose_tricycle.xml - the forklift's navigate-to-pose tree.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. nav2's default tree,
  nav2_bt_navigator/navigate_to_pose_w_replanning_and_recovery.xml, ends its
  recovery RoundRobin with

      <Spin spin_dist="1.57" .../>
      <Wait wait_duration="5.0"/>
      <BackUp backup_dist="0.30" backup_speed="0.15" .../>

  and both motion recoveries assume a base this vehicle is not.

  SPIN IS IMPOSSIBLE HERE. It commands angular.z with linear.x = 0. A
  tricycle's drive wheel stands one wheelbase ahead of the rear axle, so the
  body cannot rotate without the drive wheel travelling: there is no steer
  angle and no wheel speed that produces that twist.
  scripts/cmd_vel_to_tricycle.py REFUSES it rather than faking it - which
  means that with Spin left in the tree the vehicle would stand still for
  the whole behaviour and the behaviour would then report success.

  BACKUP IS POSSIBLE AND IS STILL REMOVED, which is the less obvious half.
  nav2_behaviors' DriveOnHeading publishes linear.x with angular.z = 0 - a
  straight line, which this vehicle drives in both directions. It is out for
  three reasons, none of them kinematic:

    - Its collision check simulates a STRAIGHT line from the current pose.
      The steer axis is wherever the controller last left it and takes up to
      0.79 s to slew back to centre (90 deg at model.sdf's 2.0 rad/s), so
      the vehicle drives an ARC through space the check never examined.
    - 0.30 m is 11 % of this vehicle's 2.735 m length. Sized to actually
      change the situation it would be metres, and a blind multi-metre
      reverse with 1.875 m of fork leading is not a thing a 900 kg machine
      should do because a plan failed.
    - Reverse here is a PLANNED manoeuvre. The Reeds-Shepp planner emits
      reverse segments and cusps against the map whenever reverse is the
      right answer. A blind reverse recovery duplicates that without the
      map, which is strictly worse.

  WHAT IS LEFT, AND IT IS THE POINT RATHER THAN A LOSS: clear the costmaps,
  wait, replan - and if it still cannot plan, STOP AND REPORT. A planner
  that refuses is better than one that silently fails or routes through a
  rack, and a tree with no motion recovery is what makes the refusal prompt
  and quiet instead of a machine milling about in an aisle.

  EVERYTHING ELSE IS THE STOCK TREE, deliberately: the 1 Hz replan rate, the
  six top-level retries, the per-branch costmap clears and the two error
  codes are nav2's and are not re-argued here.

  THIS FILE CONTAINS NO SAFETY LOGIC. The protective stop, the e-stop chain
  and safe torque off are onboard and hardwired and appear in no behaviour
  tree (invariant 1).
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="6" name="NavigateRecovery">
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
        <ControllerSelector selected_controller="{selected_controller}" default_controller="FollowPath" topic_name="controller_selector"/>
        <PlannerSelector selected_planner="{selected_planner}" default_planner="GridBased" topic_name="planner_selector"/>
        <RateController hz="0.1">
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
            <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="{selected_planner}" error_code_id="{compute_path_error_code}"/>
            <Sequence>
              <WouldAPlannerRecoveryHelp error_code="{compute_path_error_code}"/>
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
            </Sequence>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="{selected_controller}" error_code_id="{follow_path_error_code}"/>
          <Sequence>
            <WouldAControllerRecoveryHelp error_code="{follow_path_error_code}"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
          </Sequence>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>
      <Sequence>
        <Fallback>
          <WouldAControllerRecoveryHelp error_code="{follow_path_error_code}"/>
          <WouldAPlannerRecoveryHelp error_code="{compute_path_error_code}"/>
        </Fallback>
        <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
          <GoalUpdated/>
          <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
            <Sequence name="ClearingActions">
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
            </Sequence>
            <Wait wait_duration="5.0"/>
          </RoundRobin>
        </ReactiveFallback>
      </Sequence>
    </RecoveryNode>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
